RISC-V mathvec (downstream development ABI)
===========================================

This is an opt-in implementation for RV64/lp64d.  The baseline libc may be
built for rv64gc; only libmvec and the RVV test adapters use rv64gcv.  A
caller must have RVV 1.0 and VLEN >= 128.  The delivered set is the five
binary64 functions log, exp, sin, cos and pow, each with independent LMUL 1,
2, 4 and 8 entries: the m2 double entry _ZGV2N4v_log passes and receives an
LMUL=2 vector of four doubles, the m1 entry _ZGV1N2v_log an LMUL=1 vector of
two doubles.  The names follow the current RISC-V vector-function ABI,
without the obsolete 'r' prefix.  Unmasked scalable entries such as
_ZGV2Nxv_log and _ZGV1Nxv_log process all VLMAX lanes of their own LMUL
regardless of incoming vl; callers handle tails.  Masked entries take a mask
followed by the input vector(s).  Inactive result values are unspecified and
must not raise exceptions; see the masked notes below.

Function matrix
---------------
The matrix is 5 scalar functions x 12 variants = 60 exported symbols in
GLIBC_2.45:

  double: log exp sin cos pow

The five functions export _ZGV{1,2}N{2,4}[v]_<fn>, _ZGV{1,2}N{x}[v]_<fn>,
_ZGV{1,2}M{2,4}[v]_<fn>, _ZGV{1,2}M{x}[v]_<fn> and the unmasked m4/m8
_ZGV{4,8}N{8,16}[v]_<fn> and _ZGV{4,8}N{x}[v]_<fn>.  pow uses the binary 'vv'
suffix.  Fixed-lane counts are 2/4/8/16 for m1..m8, scalable entries use lane
marker 'x'.  The m4 and m8 masked entries are not delivered.  libmvec.abilist
lists exactly the 60 exported names, and the manifest mathvec-variants.mk is
the single source of truth for the symbol spelling, availability macro,
compiler capability macro, test name and routine.

The masked ABI puts the mask first.  All five functions integer-merge
inactive lanes to a benign pattern before any floating-point work and then
run a mask-free core: log merges to 1.0, exp, sin and cos merge to 0.0, and
pow merges both operands to 1.0.  A signaling NaN in an inactive lane
therefore never reaches classification, conversion or any floating-point
operation, and no inactive lane can raise an exception or set errno.  The
inactive result value is unspecified.  Unmasked and masked all-active results
are bit-identical because both use the same core with the same lane count.

Handshake and headers
---------------------
GLIBC_2.45 is a local downstream development symbol version.  It is not a
claim that the symbols are included in any upstream glibc release.  The
compiler handshake advertises vector math only when the compiler defines
__riscv_simd_math_abi_v1 == 1 and the installed bits/riscv-mathvec.h supplies
the per-function ABI mask macros generated from mathvec-variants.mk.  The
header additionally requires --enable-mathvec's fast-math path, RV64, lp64d
and RVV.  A mask records the exact fixed/scalable and unmasked/masked variants
the library promises.  The header attaches the registered
__riscv_simd_math_abi_v1(mask) attribute to the individual __DECL_SIMD_*
declarations; it does not restrict declarations merely because a particular
compile-time LMUL limit would not generate that clone.  An ordinary compiler,
or a mathvec-disabled installation, retains scalar declarations.  A partial
library is described by clearing only the absent mask bits; in particular,
missing m1 rows do not hide the installed m2 entries.  In a complete build
all five functions report mask 0x33ff (every unmasked and masked m1/m2 bit
plus the unmasked m4/m8 bits), and the legacy eight-bit subset is 0xff.

Algorithms and provenance
-------------------------
The register kernels and constant data originate from rvv-bench revision
b63f4ae6a54d162b362664f24e55a4b430a9a0c5 under
single/libmvec/oldarchive.  They are RVV adaptations of GNU libc dbl-64
e_log.c/e_log_data.c, e_exp.c/e_exp_data.c, e_pow.c/e_pow_log_data.c and the
IBM Accurate Mathematical Library s_sin.c/usncs.h/sincostab.c.  This library
replaces the benchmark array wrappers with register cores plus fixed,
scalable and masked ABI wrappers: log, exp and pow keep the benchmark's
cold-lane dispatch and the log m1 kernel keeps its multiply-add operand
order, while sin and cos keep the baseline kernel and its per-lane scalar
fixups for |x| >= 2^26, Inf and NaN.  The wrappers select nearest rounding
around the shift-based reductions, preserve accrued exception flags and
restore the caller's mode; see rvv-kernel-provenance.md for the file-level
mapping.

All kernels temporarily switch the floating-point rounding mode to nearest
and restore the caller's mode, preserving existing accrued exception flags.
Their answers are not promised to follow non-nearest caller rounding modes.
No real-hardware performance claim is made in this file.

Layout
------
This directory is the RV64/lp64d mathvec sysdeps directory, added to the
sysdeps list by riscv/rv64/rvd/Implies.  It needs a different ISA for
libmvec than for the baseline libc, and keeping the vector code in its own
sysdeps directory keeps the vector compiler flags off everything else.  The
kernels, the constant tables they include and the generic-math adapters
share the directory, the way sysdeps/aarch64/fpu keeps its vector sources,
data tables and test adapters together.  It is a sibling of riscv/rvv rather
than part of it: riscv/rvv holds the vector string and memory routines, which
are built for a vector baseline, while here only libmvec and the test
adapters use -march=rv64gcv and the baseline libc stays rv64gc.

Build-time scripts
------------------
Two generators under scripts/ are invoked by this directory's Makefile and are
part of the build, not developer tooling:

  scripts/gen-mathvec-header.py
	Writes bits/riscv-mathvec.h (availability and per-function mask macros)
	and bits/riscv-mathvec-decls.h (__DECL_SIMD_* blocks).

  scripts/gen-mathvec-fortran.py
	Writes the versioned Fortran preincludes.  It is also the manifest
	parser loaded by the other generator, so both share one contract.

The header generator re-checks the manifest on every build: the (version,
symbol) set must equal the one in Versions and libmvec.abilist, every routine
must define the symbol its row declares, and every generic-math adapter must
call it.  A manifest edited without the matching source or ABI change fails
the build instead of installing a header that disagrees with the library.
Pass --no-export-check only when running the generator by hand against a
manifest that is not part of a source tree.

Fortran and tests
-----------------
The versioned Fortran log-m2-v1 preinclude continues to promise only the fixed
and scalable double log entries.  The rvv-m2-v2 preinclude promises the five
unmasked fixed and scalable m2 families log, exp, sin, cos and pow.
Generation fails unless each family has exactly one fixed and one scalable N
m2 manifest entry with the expected precision, arity, lanes and symbol, so a
partially assembled m2 library cannot be advertised.  Every row is checked
against the canonical function/precision/arity table and two rows resolving to
the same ABI bit are rejected instead of being ORed into one mask.  The
rvv-m1-v3 preinclude states one directive per function with the exact mask;
the delivered manifest yields 255 for each of the five scalars.  A
mathvec-disabled build installs all generated files with comments only; they
remain valid no-SIMD contracts rather than falling back to the log-only v1
file.

Two C headers are generated: bits/riscv-mathvec.h carries the availability and
per-function mask macros, bits/riscv-mathvec-decls.h carries the __DECL_SIMD_*
blocks.  math-vector.h includes them only on the new handshake path and keeps
the legacy hand-written m2-only block for old compilers.  GCC's
riscv_simd_math_table intersects the requested mask with the entry's supported
mask and synthesizes fixed/scalable, masked/unmasked clones; the mask-first
call lowering builds the explicit leading mask argument for both unary and
binary kernels.

Each variant has a glibc generic-math test adapter built only for RVV and a
GCC testsuite entry that scans the simdclone dump for the expected symbol and
the assembler for the clone call.  The adapters cover special values, masked
inactive-lane exception behaviour and all-active lane identity.  Sampled
true-ULP maxima for the five kernels and their frozen corpus identity are
recorded in the task report; they are sampled acceptance evidence, not a
full-domain bound.

The private length-first _len ABI remains experimental: only
_ZGV{1,2}Mxv_{log,exp}_len are implemented, they are not exported, and the
riscv-mathvec-len.h opt-in is required.
